    "updated_at": "2025-08-01T18:15:58Z",
    "body": "I agree that the spec covers this.  I do think the spec could probably benefit from a non-normative block around [suspendable worker](https://securitylabs.datadoghq.com/articles/whoami-a-cloud-image-name-confusion-attack/#exploiting-the-vulnerability) that helps contextualize BFCache.\n\nIn terms of current implementation, Firefox/Gecko has a concept of freezing globals when they go into BFCache and where the way we handle workers sounds akin to what Chrome does.  Our worker implementation has a concept of high-priority Control Runnables that are drained by the event loop with top priority and also trigger our JS-interrupt mechanism that will interrupt running JS/WASM to process them (with the JS still on the stack).  The control runnable recursively tells any child workers to freeze and then pauses/blocks the worker until it is notified by its owner/parent to \"thaw\".  When a document is frozen because it goes into BFCache it tells its SharedWorkers to freeze and then the SharedWorkerManager decides to freeze the worker when every SharedWorker binding is frozen ([diagram](https://searchfox.org/mozilla-central/query/default?q=calls-between%3A%27mozilla%3A%3Adom%3A%3ARemoteWorkerController%3A%3AFreeze%27+calls-between%3A%27nsGlobalWindowInner%3A%3AFreeze%27+depth%3A8)).",
